      ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $\\sqrt{35}$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $Z$ nor to $N.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{35}\\notin N\\qquad \\sqrt{35}\\notin Z\n$$\n\n- Also, $\\sqrt{35}$ is irrational because $35$ is positive and not a perfect square. It cannot be written as the ratio of two integers, so it does *not* belong to $Q$ either:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{35}\\notin Q\n$$\n\n- Every irrational number is both a real number and a complex number. Consequently, $\\sqrt{35}$ belongs to both $R$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{35}\\in R\\qquad \\sqrt{35}\\in C\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R, C$ only."
